INSERT INTO author (name, image_url, biography) VALUES
('Virgil', '/img-author/virgil.png', 'Roman poet of the Augustan age, author of the Aeneid, which became the national epic of Rome. His work combines mythology, political ideology, and poetic refinement, portraying the foundation of Roman identity through Aeneas. Virgil’s style is highly structured, influenced by Homeric epics, but deeply shaped by Roman values such as duty, sacrifice, pietas, and imperial destiny. He worked within the cultural program of Augustus, contributing to the construction of a unified Roman identity through literature. His poetry blends technical mastery with emotional depth, often expressing a subtle tension between political celebration and tragic awareness of human fragility.'),

('Homer', '/img-author/homer.png', 'Ancient Greek poet traditionally credited with the Iliad and the Odyssey. His works represent the foundation of Western epic literature. Homeric poetry explores heroism, honor, fate, and the relationship between humans and gods in a mythological framework deeply rooted in oral tradition. The epics reflect a world governed by divine intervention and heroic codes of conduct, where human life is shaped by both personal agency and inescapable destiny. Homer’s influence extends across centuries, establishing narrative structures, archetypes, and themes that define much of later Western literature.'),

('Johann Wolfgang von Goethe', '/img-author/goethe.png', 'German writer, poet, and thinker of the Enlightenment and Romantic periods. Author of Faust, he explored themes of human ambition, knowledge, and moral conflict. Goethe combined literature, philosophy, and science, becoming one of the central figures of European intellectual history. His work bridges rational Enlightenment ideals and Romantic emotional depth, investigating the limits of human understanding and the complexity of personal development. Beyond literature, he contributed to natural sciences and aesthetics, promoting a holistic vision of human culture and intellectual growth.'),

('Fyodor Dostoevsky', '/img-author/fyodor.png', 'Russian novelist known for deep psychological and philosophical exploration of morality, guilt, and free will. His works, including Crime and Punishment, analyze human suffering, redemption, and the conflict between rational ideology and spiritual truth. Dostoevsky’s narratives often focus on extreme psychological states and moral crises, using characters as vehicles for philosophical debate. His writing is marked by intense emotional depth and a focus on existential questions concerning faith, responsibility, and the nature of evil in human society.'),

('Plato', '/img-author/plato.png', 'Ancient Greek philosopher and student of Socrates. Founder of the Academy in Athens and author of philosophical dialogues such as The Republic. He developed foundational concepts in Western philosophy including ideal forms, justice, and political theory. Plato’s thought distinguishes between the sensory world and the realm of eternal Forms, emphasizing reason as the path to truth. His works combine philosophical argumentation with dramatic dialogue, shaping the development of metaphysics, epistemology, and political philosophy in Western thought.'),

('Miguel de Cervantes', '/img-author/cervantes.png', 'Spanish writer best known for Don Quixote, considered the first modern novel. His work satirizes chivalric traditions while exploring reality, imagination, and identity. Cervantes had a major influence on narrative structure and modern literature. His writing blends humor, irony, and social critique, reflecting the complexities of Spanish Golden Age society. Through layered storytelling and metafictional techniques, he questions the boundaries between fiction and reality.'),

('Dante Alighieri', '/img-author/alighieri.png', 'Italian poet of the Middle Ages, author of The Divine Comedy. His work represents a synthesis of medieval theology, philosophy, and political thought. Dante’s vision of the afterlife became one of the most influential literary works in history. He structured reality according to a moral and metaphysical order, integrating classical philosophy with Christian doctrine. His use of the vernacular Italian language helped establish it as a literary medium of high prestige.'),

('George Orwell', '/img-author/orwell.png', 'British writer known for political novels such as 1984 and Animal Farm. His work critiques totalitarianism, propaganda, and the manipulation of truth. Orwell emphasized clarity in language and political honesty. His experiences in war and colonial contexts shaped his skepticism toward ideological systems. Through allegory and dystopian fiction, he exposed mechanisms of control, surveillance, and linguistic distortion in modern political regimes.'),

('Aldous Huxley', '/img-author/huxley.png', 'English writer and philosopher known for Brave New World. His works explore technological control, dystopian societies, and the relationship between science, freedom, and human identity. Huxley examined the psychological and social consequences of technological progress, questioning whether rational organization leads to genuine human fulfillment or subtle forms of control. His later work also reflects interest in consciousness, mysticism, and altered perception.'),

('Sun Tzu', '/img-author/tzu.png', 'Ancient Chinese military strategist and author of The Art of War. His teachings focus on strategy, adaptability, and psychological warfare, influencing military and business strategy for centuries. Sun Tzu emphasizes victory through intelligence, positioning, and minimal conflict rather than direct confrontation. His ideas extend beyond warfare into broader principles of decision-making, competition, and strategic thinking in uncertain environments.'),

('Herman Melville', '/img-author/melville.png', 'American novelist best known for Moby Dick. His work blends adventure with philosophical reflection on obsession, nature, and human limitation. Melville explores the tension between human ambition and the indifferent forces of the natural world. His narratives often question authority, meaning, and the boundaries of knowledge, using maritime settings as symbolic spaces for existential inquiry.'),

('Leo Tolstoy', '/img-author/tolstoy.png', 'Russian novelist and moral philosopher. Known for Anna Karenina and War and Peace, he explored themes of love, morality, society, and spiritual search. Tolstoy’s later thought increasingly focused on ethical simplicity, nonviolence, and spiritual reform. His works combine vast historical scope with intimate psychological detail, examining how individual lives intersect with broader social and historical forces.'),

('Albert Camus', '/img-author/camus.png', 'French philosopher and writer associated with existentialism and absurdism. His works explore meaninglessness, alienation, and human freedom in an indifferent universe. Camus developed the concept of the absurd, emphasizing the tension between human desire for meaning and the silence of the world. His philosophy advocates rebellion and solidarity as responses to existential uncertainty.'),

('Franz Kafka', '/img-author/kafka.png', 'Bohemian writer known for surreal and existential narratives. His works explore alienation, bureaucracy, and identity in oppressive systems. Kafka’s fiction portrays individuals trapped in incomprehensible structures of authority, where meaning is perpetually deferred. His style combines clarity of language with disturbing ambiguity, producing a sense of existential unease.'),

('Mary Shelley', '/img-author/shelley.png', 'English writer, author of Frankenstein. Considered a pioneer of science fiction, she explored themes of creation, responsibility, and scientific ambition. Her work reflects concerns about the limits of human knowledge and the ethical consequences of scientific experimentation. Frankenstein remains a foundational text in discussions of technology, ethics, and artificial life.'),

('Emily Brontë', '/img-author/bronte.png', 'English novelist and poet, author of Wuthering Heights. Her work explores intense passion, revenge, and social class through dark romanticism. Brontë’s narrative style combines psychological depth with Gothic atmosphere, portraying destructive emotional relationships and the tension between nature and civilization.'),

('James Joyce', '/img-author/joyce.png', 'Irish modernist writer known for Ulysses. He revolutionized narrative style using stream-of-consciousness techniques and linguistic experimentation. Joyce’s work focuses on the complexity of consciousness, urban life, and linguistic innovation. His writing breaks traditional narrative structures, emphasizing subjective experience and the multiplicity of meaning.'),

('William Shakespeare', '/img-author/shakespeare.png', 'English playwright and poet, considered the greatest writer in the English language. His works explore tragedy, comedy, politics, and human psychology. Shakespeare’s plays examine universal aspects of human behavior, including ambition, love, jealousy, and power. His language and dramatic structures have profoundly shaped Western literature and theater.'),

('Mark Twain', '/img-author/twain.png', 'American writer known for satire and adventure novels such as Huckleberry Finn. He criticized social hypocrisy and racism in 19th-century America. Twain’s writing combines humor, realism, and social critique, exposing contradictions in American society. His work remains central to discussions of race, identity, and national culture.'),

('Arthur Conan Doyle', '/img-author/doyle.png', 'British writer and physician, creator of Sherlock Holmes. He shaped the detective genre using logic, deduction, and scientific reasoning. Doyle’s stories established the modern model of investigative fiction, emphasizing rational analysis, evidence-based reasoning, and structured problem-solving. Sherlock Holmes became a cultural archetype of intellectual mastery and analytical thinking.');

